{:edb/db-before-t 2171 :edb/db-after-t 2172 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535484 50 #inst "2026-10-09T01:00:44.443Z" 13194139535484 true] [17592186115155 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351551 13194139535484 true] [17592186115156 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351558 13194139535484 true] [17592186115156 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351565 13194139535484 true] [17592186115668 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535484 true] [17592186115668 1100 "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If a $2\\times 2$-matrix $A$ is invertible, then $\\text{__________}$ . *\n\n- $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution\n- $Ax=0$ has infinitely many solutions\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$" 13194139535484 true] [17592186115668 1100 "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n> *If a $2 \\cdot 2$-matrix $A$ is invertible, then $__________$.*\n\n1. $Ax = 0$ has exactly one solution\n2. $Ax = 0$ has infinitely many solutions\n3. $det(A) \\ne 0$" 13194139535484 false] [17592186115668 1145 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNow, since the first part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($A$ is invertible), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only options I and III give us true statements:\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution.* $\\,✓$\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $det(A)\\ne 0.$* $\\,✓$" 13194139535484 true] [17592186115795 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535484 true] [17592186115795 1100 "Let $A = \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$, where $k$ is a constant such that $k \\ne - 1$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $det(A) \\ne 0$\n2. $Ax = 0$ has no solutions\n3. The lines $6x + 2y = 1$ and $-3x + ky =-2$ intersect at infinitely many points" 13194139535484 false] [17592186115795 1100 "Let $A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne -1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- $Ax=0$ has no solutions\n- The lines $6x+2y=1$ and $-3x+ky=-2$ intersect at infinitely many points" 13194139535484 true] [17592186115795 1145 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6\\cdot k-2\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =6k+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $6k+6\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne -1.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be a unique zero solution to $Ax=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statement I is true." 13194139535484 true] [17592186351551 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351552 13194139535484 true] [17592186351551 1089 "q-128124" 13194139535484 true] [17592186351551 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535484 true] [17592186351551 1100 "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)=0$\n- $A$ is invertible\n- $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b$" 13194139535484 true] [17592186351551 1113 #uuid "9c80544f-2eae-5edd-8bb7-5e25399a9d28" 13194139535484 true] [17592186351551 1145 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-(-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nFinally, only statements II and III are true." 13194139535484 true] [17592186351552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351553 13194139535484 true] [17592186351552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351554 13194139535484 true] [17592186351552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351555 13194139535484 true] [17592186351552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351556 13194139535484 true] [17592186351552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351557 13194